From 6cc37e8a249b62ebd728c78b1f72b24be90d3ef4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ming Lei <ming.lei@clyso.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 10:26:36 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] os/bluestore: admit an onode to the cache on its second
 use

Every onode loaded or created went to the front of the shard's LRU, so one
pass over many objects (scrub, backfill, writes never read back) filled the
cache with onodes used once, and the tuner let it: it counts only the
mempool half of an onode's heap cost. RGW 4 KiB PUTs on a 2-OSD cluster
with a 4 GiB target drove an OSD to 4.65 GiB, 2.8 GiB of it such onodes.

An onode now enters a bounded probation FIFO at its first use and moves to
the LRU only after it is used in two cache age bins later than the one it
came in: the touches of one op sequence fall into one bin, and a scan that
lists objects and then reads them gives one later use, not two. Probation
is trimmed first, even while the shard is under its max, but it always
holds what came in during the last four age bins and at least 1024 onodes
per shard (a backfill scan reads up to 512 objects of one PG, all in one
shard, before pushing them), so admission keeps working however small the
tuner has made the shard. The age bins rotate without the autotuner too.

Option bluestore_onode_cache_probation_ratio (default 0.1; 0 gives the old
single LRU). Perf counter onodes_probation.

With the first version of the rule (one later use): deep scrub peak RSS
4.35 -> 3.34 GiB, RGW PUT 4 KiB 4.65 -> 2.26 GiB; the RBD hot set stays
cached and throughput is within run-to-run spread.
